export const BD1={title:'The event and the 2026–27 resolution',hook:'A hospital has one ICU bed and two patients who need it. A transplant center has one liver and a waiting list of hundreds. A country has enough vaccine for a fraction of its people. Who should get the scarce resource? This year’s Biomedical Debate resolution takes a clear position on that question—and your team must be ready to argue for it and against it.',objectives:['Describe the Biomedical Debate format, from qualifying test to bracket.','State the 2026–27 resolution precisely and define its key terms.','Explain how teams are scored in the debate round.','Plan a year of preparation using the official references.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Biomedical Debate is a **team event for 3–4 members**. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes** on the annual topic. The team’s **average test score** decides which teams qualify and how they are **seeded**; the test is **not** part of the final score but is used to break ties. At ILC, usually **32 secondary** and **8 postsecondary/collegiate** teams advance to a **single-elimination debate bracket**; the next two teams are alternates.',
'In **Round Two**, two teams meet in each match. Teams **draw for affirmative or negative** when they enter the room, so you must be ready to argue **both sides**. You may bring **printed prepared materials**—folders, notes, printed pages and books—but **no props, visual aids or electronic devices**, and **no timing devices**. Prep time has been removed for 2026–27. At least **three team members must speak**, and teams that are more than five minutes late forfeit.',
'Business attire is required; teams not dressed appropriately may not be allowed to compete. There are no observers during the event.',
"Winning teams return to the holding room until called again and may not communicate with other teams while they wait.",
{t:'table',caption:'Event structure',head:['Round','Format','Purpose'],rows:[['One','50-question test, 60 minutes','Qualify and seed teams; tiebreaker'],['Two','Debate bracket, about 15 minutes per match','Determines placement'],['Materials','Printed notes, folders, books','No props, devices or timers']]}]],
['The resolution',[
'The 2026–27 resolution is: **“Limited healthcare resources should be allocated to patients with the greatest likelihood of long-term survival.”** The **affirmative** supports this statement; the **negative** opposes it. Because you do not know your side in advance, every member must understand the strongest case on **both** sides.',
'Read the resolution word by word. **“Limited healthcare resources”** could include ICU beds, ventilators, organs, drugs in shortage, vaccines, specialist appointments, staff time or public health budgets. **“Should be allocated”** is a normative claim about what ought to happen. **“Greatest likelihood of long-term survival”** is the proposed criterion—it is about **prognosis** and **time**, not just surviving the immediate crisis.',
'The phrase “greatest likelihood” invites questions: How is likelihood measured? How long is “long-term”? Does the criterion stand alone, or as one factor among several? Clear definitions shape the whole debate.',
match('Term in the resolution and possible meaning',[['Limited healthcare resources','Scarce beds, organs, drugs, vaccines or funds'],['Allocated','Distributed among competing patients or groups'],['Greatest likelihood','Highest predicted probability'],['Long-term survival','Living for years after treatment, not just surviving now'],['Affirmative','Argues the resolution should be adopted'],['Negative','Argues against the resolution']])]],
['How the debate is scored',[
'Judges score each speech. The **first speeches** are rated on **arguments and evidence (persuasiveness)**, **flow and logic** and **relevance of arguments**. The **response speeches**—the first negative and the second affirmative—are scored heavily on the quality of **counterarguments**. The **summary/rebuttal speeches** are rated on **evidence and effectiveness**, **clarification of argument** and **relevance of the rebuttal** with researched data.',
'Each side is also scored on **overall debate qualities**: **voice**, **stage presence**, **diction, pronunciation and grammar**, **decorum** (professional behavior toward the other team) and **team participation**—ideally three members speaking with equal knowledge. Judges note table behavior throughout, so stay professional even while passing notes.',
'The winning team advances in the bracket. If a match is tied, the team with the higher average test score advances.',
sort('What judges score',['Speech content','Overall qualities'],[['Persuasiveness of arguments and evidence',0],['Voice and volume',1],['Accuracy and relevance of counterarguments',0],['Decorum toward the other team',1],['Clarification in the rebuttal',0],['Equal participation of three members',1]])]],
['The official references',[
'All test questions come from five references: Becca Levy’s **Breaking the Age Code** (how beliefs about aging affect health and longevity); Greg Bognar and Iwao Hirose’s **The Ethics of Health Care Rationing**; the Lancet article **“The shared ethical framework to allocate scarce medical resources: a lesson from COVID-19”**; the explainer **“Navigating Healthcare Ethics: Resource Allocation & Fairness Explained”**; and a GESDA report on **longevity science** crossing into clinical use.',
'The references cover four themes you need for both the test and the debate: **ethical theories of rationing**, **practical allocation frameworks**, **ageism and age beliefs** and **how science is changing our ideas of age and survival**. Read each one carefully, take notes by theme and record page numbers—sample test questions cite specific pages.',
'For the debate, supplement the references with credible sources such as peer-reviewed articles, professional ethics codes and government guidance, and print key evidence for your folders.',
flip('Official references',[['Breaking the Age Code','Becca Levy; age beliefs and health'],['The Ethics of Health Care Rationing','Bognar and Hirose; theories of rationing'],['Lancet 2023 framework','Shared ethics of allocating scarce resources after COVID-19'],['Resource allocation explainer','Micro, meso and macro decisions'],['GESDA longevity report','Longevity science entering the clinic'],['Use','Every test question comes from these']])]],
['A preparation plan',[
'Strong teams prepare in stages. **Months 1–2:** read the references, build a shared glossary and summarize each chapter. **Month 3:** research beyond the references and organize evidence by argument. **Months 4–5:** write affirmative and negative cases, practice timed speeches and hold practice debates against other teams. **Final weeks:** take practice tests, refine rebuttal files and rehearse delivery.',
'Divide work but avoid silos: every member must understand both cases, because any three of you may speak and sides are drawn at random. Rotate speaking roles in practice so each person can deliver any speech.',
'Track evidence in a shared document with the claim it supports, the source, the date and a one-line quotation or statistic, then print organized folders for competition.',
order('Year-long preparation',['Read the references and build a glossary','Research and organize evidence by argument','Write affirmative and negative cases','Practice timed speeches and full debates','Take practice tests','Print organized evidence folders'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Draw day',{a:{text:'Your team enters the room and draws negative, though you had practiced the affirmative more. (Scenario invented for practice.) What helps most now?',choices:[['Your negative case and rebuttal file, prepared as carefully as the affirmative','b','Correct. Prepare both sides equally.',true],['Asking to switch sides','b','Sides are drawn; no switching.',false]]},b:{text:'A teammate wants to time speeches with a phone. Is that allowed?',choices:[['No—timing devices are not allowed; rely on the timekeeper','c','Correct.',true],['Yes, quietly','c','No timing devices of any kind.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team uses printed evidence folders and practiced timing to deliver a strong negative case.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Teams of 3–4: a 50-question qualifying test (seeds the bracket and breaks ties, not part of the final score) and a debate bracket; sides are drawn on entry; printed materials allowed, no props, devices or timers; at least three members speak.','Resolution: “Limited healthcare resources should be allocated to patients with the greatest likelihood of long-term survival”—define limited resources, allocation, likelihood and long-term survival carefully.','Judges score arguments, flow, relevance, counterarguments and rebuttals plus voice, stage presence, diction, decorum and team participation; study all five official references and prepare both sides equally.'],terms:[['Resolution','The statement a debate argues for or against.'],['Affirmative','The side that supports the resolution.'],['Negative','The side that opposes the resolution.']],mcqs:[q('How many members are on a Biomedical Debate team?','3–4','2','5–6','1','Team event.'),q('What is the Round One test used for?','Qualifying and seeding teams, and breaking ties','50% of the final score','Choosing the topic','Nothing','Not part of the final score.'),q('How are sides assigned in Round Two?','Teams draw for affirmative or negative on entry','Teams choose in advance','Judges assign by test score','Coin toss in the hallway','Prepare both.'),q('Which materials are allowed in the debate?','Printed notes and folders','A laptop','A poster','A stopwatch','Hard copy only.'),q('How many team members must speak?','At least three','One','Exactly two','All six','Team participation.'),q('What does the affirmative do?','Supports the resolution','Opposes the resolution','Times the debate','Judges the round','Pro side.'),q('Which reference discusses age beliefs and longevity?','Breaking the Age Code','The Ethics of Health Care Rationing','The OPTN website','A GESDA radar report only','Becca Levy.'),q('What breaks a tie in a debate match?','The higher average Round One test score','A coin toss','The longer speech','The judges’ favorite','Tiebreaker rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Biomedical Debate format and why your team must prepare both an affirmative and a negative case on the 2026–27 resolution.',rubric:[['Format',['3-4','test','50','qualify','seed','bracket','not part']],['Debate rules',['draw','affirmative','negative','printed','no props','no timing','three members']],['Resolution analysis',['limited','allocated','likelihood','long-term survival','define']],['Preparation',['both sides','references','evidence','rotate','practice']]],model:'Biomedical Debate is a team event for three or four members. In Round One, each member takes a 50-question test on the annual topic in 60 minutes, and the team’s average score determines which teams qualify for the debate bracket and how they are seeded; the test does not count in the final score but breaks ties. In Round Two, two teams debate in a single-elimination bracket. When they enter the room, teams draw for affirmative or negative, so neither knows its side in advance. Teams may bring printed materials such as evidence folders and books, but no props, electronic devices or timing devices, and at least three members must speak. The 2026–27 resolution states that limited healthcare resources should be allocated to patients with the greatest likelihood of long-term survival. Each phrase needs careful definition: limited resources might be ICU beds, organs or drugs in shortage; “greatest likelihood” raises questions about how prognosis is measured; and “long-term survival” focuses on years of life rather than surviving the immediate crisis. Because the side is drawn at random, our team must prepare an affirmative case built on maximizing benefits and stewardship and a negative case built on equity, uncertainty and the risk of ageism and disability discrimination. Every member should know both cases and be able to give any speech, so we rotate roles in practice and organize printed evidence for both sides.'},sources:[RULES,RES_ADV,LANCET_FW]};

export const BD2={title:'Scarcity and levels of resource allocation',hook:'Every health system rations, whether it admits it or not. Waiting lists, insurance rules, staffing levels and budgets all decide who gets what and when. Understanding where allocation decisions are made—and who makes them—lets you show judges exactly what the resolution would change.',objectives:['Explain scarcity and why rationing is unavoidable in health care.','Distinguish micro-, meso- and macro-level allocation decisions.','Distinguish explicit and implicit rationing.','Identify the types of resources the resolution could cover.'],sections:[
['Scarcity in health care',[
'**Scarcity** means that demand for a resource exceeds its supply. In health care, some resources are **absolutely scarce**—there are far fewer donor organs than patients who need them. Others become scarce in **emergencies**, such as ventilators and ICU beds during a pandemic, or during **drug shortages**, such as shortages of certain chemotherapy drugs in 2023. Still others are limited by **budgets**: there is never enough money to fund every possible treatment.',
'Because resources are finite, choices are unavoidable. Even a decision to use “first come, first served” is a choice about allocation. The ethical question is not **whether** to ration, but **how** to do it fairly, transparently and consistently.',
'The resolution proposes one answer: prioritize the patients most likely to survive long-term. Both teams must engage with the reality of scarcity rather than pretending all patients can be treated.',
sort('Type of scarcity',['Absolute scarcity','Emergency scarcity','Budget limit'],[['Donor kidneys for transplant',0],['Ventilators during a pandemic surge',1],['A state choosing which programs to fund',2],['Donor hearts',0],['ICU beds after a mass-casualty event',1],['An insurer deciding which drugs to cover',2]])]],
['Micro, meso and macro decisions',[
'Allocation happens at three levels. **Micro-level** decisions concern **individual patients**: which patient receives an earlier MRI appointment, the last ICU bed or a particular organ. **Meso-level** decisions are made by **institutions**: which hospital department receives the most budget cuts, how many ICU beds a hospital staffs or which drugs a hospital stocks. **Macro-level** decisions are made by **governments and large systems**: how much a state spends on prevention versus chronic care, where to build a new veterans’ hospital or national vaccine priorities.',
'The resolution is most often discussed at the **micro** level (bedside triage), but a strong debater shows how it would play out at **meso** and **macro** levels too—for example, a hospital funding programs with better long-term survival outcomes or a government prioritizing interventions with the most life-years gained.',
'Clarifying the level helps prevent the debate from becoming two teams talking past each other.',
match('Decision and level',[['Which patient gets an earlier MRI','Micro'],['Which hospital department has the largest budget cut','Meso'],['How a state divides funding between prevention and chronic care','Macro'],['Which patient receives the last ventilator','Micro'],['How many ICU beds a hospital staffs','Meso'],['Where a new veterans’ hospital is built','Macro']])]],
['Explicit and implicit rationing',[
'**Explicit rationing** uses clear, public rules—such as organ allocation policies or published crisis standards of care. **Implicit rationing** happens through less visible mechanisms: waiting lists, clinician judgment, cost-sharing, travel distance, insurance coverage and staffing shortages.',
'Explicit rationing is more **transparent** and **accountable** but can be politically difficult because it names who will not be served. Implicit rationing can hide unfairness—for example, people who cannot take time off work may effectively lose access. Both sides of the debate can use this distinction: the affirmative can argue that a clear survival-based rule is more transparent than hidden rationing; the negative can argue that a survival rule simply makes existing inequities explicit and official.',
'Ask of any proposal: Who decides? Using what information? Can the public see and challenge the rule?',
sort('Explicit or implicit rationing?',['Explicit','Implicit'],[['A published organ allocation policy',0],['Long waiting lists for specialists',1],['A state crisis standards of care document',0],['High copays that discourage care',1],['A hospital’s written ventilator triage protocol',0],['A clinic too far away to reach without a car',1]])]],
['Resources the resolution could cover',[
'The resolution says “limited healthcare resources” without listing them, so both teams can argue about scope. **Bedside resources**: ICU beds, ventilators, ECMO machines, dialysis slots and blood products. **Transplantable organs**: livers, hearts, kidneys and lungs. **Medications**: drugs in shortage and expensive therapies. **Prevention**: vaccines and antivirals during shortages. **Human resources**: specialist time and nursing staff. **Funding**: public health and research budgets.',
'The survival criterion fits some resources better than others. For organ transplants, long-term graft and patient survival is already one consideration. For vaccines, the goal is often preventing death and spread, so priority usually goes to people at **highest risk**, not those most likely to survive. Each team can choose examples that favor its side—and anticipate examples the other side will use.',
'Prepare a list of resources with notes on how the survival criterion would apply to each.',
chk('For which resource does prioritizing those most likely to survive long-term seem to conflict with common vaccine policy?',['Vaccines during a shortage, which usually prioritize people at highest risk of severe illness','Donor livers','ICU beds','Transplant kidneys'],'Vaccine priority often goes to high-risk groups, a strong negative example.')]],
['Stakeholders in allocation',[
'Allocation decisions affect and involve many stakeholders: **patients and families**, **clinicians** (who may face moral distress), **hospital administrators**, **ethics committees**, **public health officials**, **insurers**, **governments** and **communities**, especially groups that have faced discrimination. Each brings different values and interests.',
'Many frameworks recommend that triage decisions be made by a **triage officer or committee** separate from the treating clinician, to reduce bias and protect the patient–clinician relationship. Public engagement in creating rules can increase **legitimacy** and **trust**.',
'In the debate, considering stakeholders shows depth: How would a survival-based rule affect clinicians’ moral distress, public trust and communities with lower average life expectancy?',
match('Stakeholder and concern',[['Patients and families','Fair access and dignity'],['Clinicians','Moral distress at the bedside'],['Triage committee','Consistent, unbiased decisions'],['Hospital administrators','Capacity and resources'],['Public health officials','Population outcomes'],['Marginalized communities','Risk of discrimination']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Classifying an example',{a:{text:'An opponent says, “The resolution would decide which hospitals get funding.” (Scenario invented for practice.) Which level is this?',choices:[['Meso or macro, depending on who decides','b','Correct.',true],['Micro','b','Micro concerns individual patients.',false]]},b:{text:'How can you respond if you are affirmative?',choices:[['Clarify that the resolution is about patient-level allocation, then show it can still guide institutions consistently','c','Correct. Define scope.',true],['Ignore it','c','Address scope directly.',false]]},c:{text:'The judges see your team control the definitions of the debate.',end:true}})]],
["Opportunity costs and fairness over time",["Every allocation has an **opportunity cost**: the benefit lost by not using the resource another way. A ventilator used for one patient cannot be used for another; a dollar spent on one program cannot fund a different one. Economists and ethicists use this idea to argue that ignoring outcomes has hidden costs—people who could have been saved.","Allocation also has a **time dimension**. A patient who needs a ventilator for three weeks uses resources that could help several patients needing it for a few days. Some crisis protocols considered **expected duration of need**, which raises its own fairness concerns because sicker or disabled patients may need longer treatment.","Debaters can use opportunity cost to support the affirmative and duration concerns to support the negative. Both show judges that you understand how scarcity works in practice.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Concept and example","pairs":[["Opportunity cost","A ventilator used for one patient is unavailable to another"],["Duration of need","Three weeks on a ventilator versus three days"],["Reassessment","Reviewing whether a patient still benefits"],["Withdrawal","Reallocating a resource from a patient not improving"],["Surge capacity","Expanding resources during a crisis"],["Hidden cost","Lives not saved by an inefficient choice"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Scarcity is unavoidable—absolute (organs), emergency (ventilators, ICU beds) and budget-driven—so the question is how to ration fairly, not whether.','Micro decisions concern individual patients (an earlier MRI), meso decisions institutions (department budget cuts) and macro decisions governments (prevention vs chronic care, where to build a hospital).','Explicit rationing uses public rules; implicit rationing hides in waiting lists and costs; the resolution’s scope can include beds, organs, drugs, vaccines, staff and funding; consider stakeholders and triage committees.'],terms:[['Scarcity','When demand for a resource exceeds supply.'],['Meso-level decision','An allocation decision made by an institution such as a hospital.'],['Explicit rationing','Allocation using clear, public rules.']],mcqs:[q('What is an example of a meso-level decision for allocation of limited medical resources?','Which department of the hospital will have the most budget cuts?','Which patient receives an earlier appointment for an MRI?','How much funding by a state is allocated for preventative versus chronic care?','Which area of the country will be selected for a new veteran’s hospital?','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which is a micro-level decision?','Which patient receives the last ventilator','A national vaccine policy','A state budget','Where to build a hospital','Individual patients.'),q('Which is a macro-level decision?','A state dividing funding between prevention and chronic care','Choosing between two ICU patients','A nurse’s shift schedule','One patient’s MRI time','Government level.'),q('A published organ allocation policy is an example of:','Explicit rationing','Implicit rationing','No rationing','Random rationing','Public rules.'),q('Long specialist waiting lists are an example of:','Implicit rationing','Explicit rationing','Macro planning only','Triage officers','Hidden mechanisms.'),q('Donor organs are an example of:','Absolute scarcity','Budget scarcity only','No scarcity','Temporary surplus','Far fewer than needed.'),q('Why do many frameworks use a separate triage committee?','To reduce bias and protect the clinician–patient relationship','To speed up paperwork only','To replace all doctors','To avoid rules','Consistency.'),q('During vaccine shortages, priority usually goes to:','People at highest risk of severe illness','Those most likely to survive anyway','Only the youngest','Only clinicians’ families','A negative example.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain micro-, meso- and macro-level allocation decisions with examples, and describe how this framework could help either side in the debate.',rubric:[['Micro',['micro','individual','patient','mri','ventilator','bed']],['Meso',['meso','institution','hospital','department','budget']],['Macro',['macro','government','state','national','prevention','veterans']],['Debate use',['scope','define','affirmative','negative','consistent','explicit','implicit']]],model:'Resource allocation happens at three levels. Micro-level decisions involve individual patients, such as which patient receives an earlier MRI appointment, the last ICU bed or a donated liver. Meso-level decisions are made by institutions, such as a hospital deciding which department faces the largest budget cut, how many ICU beds to staff or which costly drugs to stock. Macro-level decisions are made by governments and large systems, such as how a state divides funding between preventive and chronic care, national vaccine priorities or where to build a new veterans’ hospital. This framework helps both sides control the debate. The affirmative can define the resolution mainly at the micro level—bedside triage during shortages—where prognosis-based rules already appear in organ allocation and crisis standards of care, and argue that a clear survival-based rule is more transparent than implicit rationing through waiting lists and costs. The affirmative can also show that the same principle guides meso and macro choices consistently by funding interventions that produce the most years of life. The negative can use the framework to expand the scope and show unintended effects: at the macro level, prioritizing groups with the best long-term survival could steer money away from communities with lower life expectancy because of poverty or discrimination, and at the meso level hospitals might avoid programs for older or disabled patients. Clarifying the level prevents teams from talking past each other and shows judges a deep understanding of the topic.'},sources:[RULES,RES_ADV,NASEM_CSC]};

export const BD3={title:'Ethical theories of rationing',hook:'Behind every allocation rule is a theory of fairness. Should we do the most good for the most people? Treat everyone equally? Help the worst off first? The Ethics of Health Care Rationing—one of your official references—explores these theories, and judges will reward debaters who can name the theory behind an argument and its weaknesses.',objectives:['Explain utilitarianism and how it supports maximizing benefits.','Explain egalitarian, prioritarian and sufficientarian views.','Apply the four principles of biomedical ethics to allocation.','Identify strengths and weaknesses of each theory for the resolution.'],sections:[
['Utilitarianism and maximizing benefits',[
'**Utilitarianism** holds that the right action produces the greatest overall good. In health care, “good” is often measured as **lives saved**, **life-years saved** or **quality-adjusted life-years (QALYs)**. A utilitarian would allocate a scarce resource where it produces the **largest health benefit**—which often means giving it to patients most likely to survive and live longest.',
'This theory strongly supports the **affirmative**: prioritizing those with the greatest likelihood of long-term survival maximizes the benefit from each scarce resource. Critics note that utilitarianism can ignore **how benefits are distributed**, potentially disadvantaging people who are older, disabled or chronically ill, and that it can treat individuals merely as means to aggregate outcomes.',
'Bognar and Hirose discuss how maximizing approaches face the **aggregation problem**: should many small benefits outweigh one large benefit? Debaters should be ready for this challenge.',
flip('Utilitarian measures',[['Lives saved','Count of people who survive'],['Life-years saved','Total additional years of life'],['QALY','A year of life adjusted for quality of health'],['Maximizing','Choosing the option with the most benefit'],['Aggregation problem','Do many small benefits outweigh one large one?'],['Main critique','Ignores fair distribution']])]],
['Egalitarianism and equal respect',[
'**Egalitarian** views emphasize that every person has **equal moral worth** and should have an **equal chance** at scarce resources. Practical expressions include a **lottery** or **first come, first served**. Egalitarians worry that survival-based rules make people’s access depend on characteristics—such as age, disability or prior illness—that are often outside their control.',
'This theory supports the **negative**, especially arguments that a survival criterion discriminates against older adults and people with disabilities. Critics reply that strict equality can **waste** scarce resources on patients unlikely to benefit, leading to more deaths overall. Some egalitarians accept prognosis as a threshold—excluding only those very unlikely to benefit—while rejecting fine-grained ranking.',
'Note that “first come, first served” often favors people with better access to care, so it may not be truly equal in practice.',
sort('Supports the affirmative or the negative?',['Affirmative','Negative'],[['Maximize life-years saved',0],['Every person deserves an equal chance',1],['Do not waste scarce ventilators',0],['Lottery among eligible patients',1],['QALYs guide allocation',0],['Avoid ranking people by predicted lifespan',1]])]],
['Prioritarianism and sufficientarianism',[
'**Prioritarianism** gives **extra weight to the worst off**: benefits to people who are sicker or more disadvantaged count more. A prioritarian might prioritize the **sickest patients** or communities facing the greatest burden of disease—which may conflict with the resolution, because the sickest are often least likely to survive long-term.',
'**Sufficientarianism** holds that what matters is ensuring everyone reaches a **threshold** of health or life. The **“fair innings” argument** is related: everyone deserves a chance to live a full lifespan, so younger people who have had fewer years should get priority. Interestingly, fair innings can support the **affirmative** in practice (younger patients often have better long-term survival) while resting on a different rationale—**fairness across lifetimes**, not prognosis.',
'Knowing these nuances lets debaters agree with an opponent’s conclusion while challenging their reasoning—a powerful rebuttal technique.',
"Libertarian views add another angle: some argue individuals should be free to buy care privately, which raises questions about whether wealth should influence access. Most allocation frameworks reject ability to pay as a criterion for life-saving resources during crises.",
match('Theory and key idea',[['Utilitarianism','Maximize total benefit'],['Egalitarianism','Equal chance and equal worth'],['Prioritarianism','Extra weight to the worst off'],['Sufficientarianism','Everyone reaches a threshold'],['Fair innings','Priority to those who have lived fewer years'],['Contractualism','Rules no one could reasonably reject']])]],
['The four principles of biomedical ethics',[
'Beauchamp and Childress describe four principles widely used in health care: **autonomy** (respecting patients’ choices), **beneficence** (doing good), **nonmaleficence** (avoiding harm) and **justice** (fair distribution of benefits and burdens). Allocation debates center on **justice**, but the others matter too.',
'**Beneficence** can support the affirmative—using resources where they do the most good. **Justice** can support either side: **distributive justice** asks what fair distribution looks like, and people disagree about whether maximizing survival or equal chance is fairer. **Nonmaleficence** raises concerns about withdrawing treatment from patients who are not prioritized. **Autonomy** suggests patients’ values and advance directives matter.',
'Using the principles fluently makes speeches sound grounded in medical ethics rather than opinion.',
match('Principle and allocation application',[['Beneficence','Use resources where they do the most good'],['Justice','Distribute benefits and burdens fairly'],['Nonmaleficence','Avoid harm from withdrawing care'],['Autonomy','Respect patients’ values and directives'],['Distributive justice','Fair allocation of scarce goods'],['Procedural justice','Fair, transparent decision process']])]],
['Procedural fairness',[
'Even when people disagree about **which** criteria are best, they may agree on **how** decisions should be made. Daniels and Sabin’s **accountability for reasonableness** framework says allocation rules should be **public** (publicity), based on reasons stakeholders accept as relevant (**relevance**), open to **appeal and revision**, and **enforced**. The Lancet 2023 framework similarly emphasizes shared principles and transparent processes learned from COVID-19.',
'Procedural fairness gives debaters a strong middle ground. The affirmative can argue that a clear survival criterion, published in advance and applied by triage committees, satisfies procedural fairness. The negative can argue that no procedure can make an unjust criterion fair, or that prognosis tools hide bias inside seemingly objective scores.',
'Whatever your side, address **who decides**, **how** and **how decisions can be challenged**.',
order('Accountability for reasonableness',['Publicity: rules are public','Relevance: reasons stakeholders accept','Revision and appeals: decisions can be challenged','Enforcement: the process is followed'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
ethics('Reason through a fair-allocation decision',2),
kase('Naming the theory',{a:{text:'The negative argues, “A 75-year-old has the same right to a ventilator as a 25-year-old.” (Scenario invented for practice.) Which theory is this?',choices:[['Egalitarianism','b','Correct. Equal worth and equal chance.',true],['Utilitarianism','b','Utilitarianism maximizes total benefit.',false]]},b:{text:'How might the affirmative respond?',choices:[['Agree that worth is equal, but argue that equal respect allows using prognosis to save the most life-years','c','Correct. Concede, then reframe.',true],['Say older people matter less','c','That would be disrespectful and weak.',false]]},c:{text:'The judges reward the respectful, theory-based response.',end:true}})]],
["Rationing and human rights",["International human rights law recognizes a **right to health**—the highest attainable standard of physical and mental health—which governments must realize **progressively** within available resources while avoiding **discrimination**. This framework accepts that resources are limited but insists that allocation must not disadvantage people because of age, disability, race or other protected characteristics.","The negative can use human rights to argue that ranking patients by predicted long-term survival risks indirect discrimination. The affirmative can argue that using resources where they save the most lives fulfills governments’ duty to make the best use of limited resources.","Bring rights language into speeches carefully and accurately; judges reward precise use of concepts.",{"t":"check","q":"Which idea from human rights law is most relevant to allocation?","options":["The right to health must be realized without discrimination within available resources","Health care must be unlimited","Rights do not apply to health","Only citizens have health rights"],"why":"Progressive realization and non-discrimination."}]]
],takeaways:['Utilitarianism maximizes benefit (lives, life-years, QALYs) and supports the affirmative but can ignore distribution and disadvantage older, disabled or chronically ill people.','Egalitarianism (equal chance, lotteries) supports the negative; prioritarianism favors the worst off (often the sickest); sufficientarianism and fair innings favor reaching a full lifespan.','Apply autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence and justice; procedural fairness (accountability for reasonableness: publicity, relevance, appeals, enforcement) asks who decides and how.'],terms:[['Utilitarianism','The view that the right action maximizes overall good.'],['Prioritarianism','Giving extra weight to benefits for the worst off.'],['QALY','A quality-adjusted life-year, combining length and quality of life.']],mcqs:[q('Which theory most directly supports maximizing life-years saved?','Utilitarianism','Egalitarianism','Prioritarianism','Libertarianism','Greatest good.'),q('A lottery among eligible patients reflects:','Egalitarianism','Utilitarianism','Fair innings only','Ageism','Equal chance.'),q('Which theory gives extra weight to the worst off?','Prioritarianism','Utilitarianism','Sufficientarianism','Egoism','Sickest first.'),q('The fair innings argument gives priority to:','People who have lived fewer years','The oldest patients','The wealthiest','Clinicians','Full lifespan.'),q('Which principle concerns fair distribution of benefits and burdens?','Justice','Autonomy','Nonmaleficence','Fidelity','Distributive justice.'),q('A QALY measures:','Length of life adjusted for quality','Cost only','Hospital size','Number of doctors','Quality-adjusted life-year.'),q('Which is part of accountability for reasonableness?','Rules are public and open to appeal','Rules are secret','Only doctors decide privately','No appeals','Procedural fairness.'),q('A common critique of utilitarian allocation is that it:','Can ignore how benefits are distributed','Always helps the sickest','Uses lotteries','Ignores outcomes','Distribution matters.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare utilitarian and egalitarian approaches to allocating scarce resources, explain which side of the resolution each supports and describe one weakness of each.',rubric:[['Utilitarian',['utilitarian','maximize','life-years','qaly','benefit']],['Egalitarian',['egalitarian','equal','lottery','first come','worth']],['Sides',['affirmative','negative','supports','resolution']],['Weaknesses',['distribution','older','disabled','waste','fewer lives','access']]],model:'Utilitarianism holds that the right choice produces the greatest overall good, which in health care is often measured as lives saved, life-years saved or quality-adjusted life-years. A utilitarian would give a scarce ventilator or organ to the patient expected to gain the most benefit, which usually means the patient with the greatest likelihood of long-term survival, so utilitarianism strongly supports the affirmative. Its weakness is that it focuses on the total amount of benefit and can ignore how benefits are distributed: older adults, people with disabilities and people with chronic illnesses—often through no fault of their own—may be systematically ranked lower, and individuals can feel treated as means to an aggregate goal. Egalitarianism holds that every person has equal moral worth and should have an equal chance at scarce resources, so it favors methods such as lotteries or first come, first served and supports the negative, which argues that ranking people by predicted survival makes access depend on characteristics like age or disability. Its weakness is that strict equality can waste scarce resources on patients very unlikely to benefit, potentially causing more deaths overall, and that first come, first served often favors people with better access to care, so it may not be equal in practice. Many real frameworks blend the two, using prognosis to exclude patients very unlikely to benefit while using lotteries or other fair methods among the rest.'},sources:[RULES,SEP_JUSTICE,PERSAD,LANCET_FW]};

export const BD4={title:'Allocation criteria and frameworks',hook:'Should the last ICU bed go to the patient who arrived first, the sickest patient, the youngest, a health care worker or the one most likely to survive? Ethicists have studied each criterion carefully. Knowing them—and the frameworks that combine them—gives your team the vocabulary and evidence to argue either side with precision.',objectives:['Describe major allocation criteria and their rationales.','Explain the Persad and Emanuel frameworks for scarce resources.','Explain how prognosis is estimated and its limitations.','Evaluate where the resolution’s criterion fits among the alternatives.'],sections:[
['Common allocation criteria',[
'Persad, Wertheimer and Emanuel (2009) grouped allocation principles into four families. **Treating people equally**: lottery, first come, first served. **Favoring the worst off**: sickest first, youngest first. **Maximizing total benefits**: save the most lives, save the most life-years, prognosis. **Promoting and rewarding social usefulness**: instrumental value (people who can help others, such as health care workers) and reciprocity (people who have contributed, such as organ donors or research participants).',
'They concluded that **no single principle is sufficient** and recommended a **complete lives system** that combines several principles: youngest first (but not infants, in their view), prognosis, save the most lives and lottery. The resolution’s survival criterion most closely matches **prognosis** and **saving the most life-years**.',
'Knowing the full menu lets you show judges what the alternative to the resolution would be—a key move for the negative.',
sort('Criterion family',['Treat equally','Favor worst off','Maximize benefits','Social usefulness'],[['Lottery',0],['Sickest first',1],['Save the most life-years',2],['Prioritize health care workers',3],['First come, first served',0],['Prognosis',2]])]],
['The Emanuel COVID-19 framework',[
'In 2020, Emanuel and colleagues proposed **four fundamental values** for allocating scarce resources during COVID-19: **maximizing the benefits** produced by scarce resources, **treating people equally**, **promoting and rewarding instrumental value** and **giving priority to the worst off**. They recommended: maximize benefits (save the most lives and, secondarily, most life-years); prioritize health care workers; do **not** use first come, first served; respond to evidence; recognize research participation; and apply the same principles to COVID-19 and non-COVID-19 patients.',
'They also advised that for patients with similar prognoses, equality should be invoked through **random allocation** (a lottery) rather than first come, first served. This combination—prognosis first, then lottery among equals—is a useful middle position for both teams to understand.',
'The affirmative can cite this widely referenced framework as support for prioritizing benefit; the negative can point out that the authors did not rely on long-term survival alone and combined it with other values.',
order('A blended triage approach',['Assess each patient’s prognosis','Exclude only those very unlikely to benefit','Prioritize saving the most lives','Consider life-years as a secondary factor','Use a lottery among patients with similar priority','Reassess regularly and allow appeals'])]],
['Measuring prognosis',[
'To prioritize “greatest likelihood of long-term survival,” clinicians need ways to predict outcomes. In ICUs, scores such as **SOFA** (Sequential Organ Failure Assessment) estimate short-term mortality from organ function. In transplantation, scores like **MELD** (for liver disease urgency) and **EPTS/KDPI** (estimated post-transplant survival and kidney donor profile index for kidney **longevity matching**) shape allocation. Some crisis protocols added **comorbidity** assessments to estimate longer-term survival.',
'Prognostic tools have limits. SOFA was designed to describe illness severity, not to predict long-term survival, and some studies found it performed poorly for COVID-19 triage. Tools can reflect **bias**—for example, kidney function equations that once used race coefficients—and can disadvantage people whose baseline scores reflect **chronic disability** rather than acute illness.',
'Both teams should understand these tools: the affirmative to show the criterion is workable; the negative to show its uncertainty and potential bias.',
match('Tool and use',[['SOFA score','ICU organ failure severity'],['MELD score','Liver transplant urgency'],['EPTS','Estimated post-transplant survival for kidney candidates'],['KDPI','Quality and expected longevity of a donor kidney'],['Comorbidity assessment','Estimating longer-term survival'],['Race coefficient controversy','Bias built into clinical equations']])]],
['Short-term versus long-term survival',[
'Many crisis standards focus on **short-term survival**—surviving to hospital discharge—because it is more predictable and less likely to discriminate. Adding **long-term survival** (for example, survival beyond one or five years) raises the stakes: it can more strongly disadvantage people with chronic conditions, older adults and those with disabilities, because these factors affect life expectancy more than they affect surviving the immediate crisis.',
'The resolution explicitly chooses **long-term** survival. The affirmative argues this maximizes the years of life gained from each scarce resource and avoids investing heavily in patients who may survive briefly only to die soon after. The negative argues that long-term predictions are **less accurate**, invite **discrimination** and conflict with guidance that warns against judging people by their long-term life expectancy.',
'Defining “long-term” is strategic: a team that sets a reasonable definition early often controls the debate.',
sort('Fits short-term or long-term survival?',['Short-term survival','Long-term survival'],[['Surviving to hospital discharge',0],['Living five years after transplant',1],['Surviving the next 30 days',0],['Expected remaining lifespan',1],['SOFA-based ICU triage',0],['Kidney longevity matching',1]])]],
['Legal and professional guidance',[
'Professional and legal guidance shapes what allocation rules can do. The **AMA Code of Medical Ethics** says allocation decisions should be based on ethically sound criteria such as **likelihood and amount of benefit**, **urgency of need** and, in some cases, the **amount of resources** required—and should avoid criteria based on social worth. During COVID-19, the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services **Office for Civil Rights** advised that crisis standards must not discriminate on the basis of **disability** or **age** and should rely on individualized assessments of short-term survival rather than stereotypes or long-term life expectancy.',
'This guidance is powerful **negative** evidence against using long-term survival. The affirmative can respond that likelihood of benefit is an accepted criterion and that the resolution can be implemented with individualized, evidence-based assessments rather than stereotypes.',
'Always cite guidance accurately and with its date; policies change over time.',
chk('Which criterion does the AMA Code of Medical Ethics list as appropriate for allocation?',['Likelihood and amount of benefit','Social worth','Ability to pay','Celebrity status'],'Benefit and urgency are accepted criteria; social worth is not.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Two patients, one ventilator',{a:{text:'Two patients need the last ventilator. Patient A has a 70% chance of surviving to discharge; Patient B has 40%. (Scenario invented for practice.) What does a prognosis criterion suggest?',choices:[['Patient A','b','Correct.',true],['Patient B','b','A has the better predicted outcome.',false]]},b:{text:'Now both have similar short-term prognoses, but Patient B has a chronic disability that may shorten long-term survival. What would many frameworks do?',choices:[['Treat them as similar and use a lottery, avoiding long-term life expectancy based on disability','c','Correct.',true],['Automatically choose Patient A','c','That would weigh disability-related life expectancy.',false]]},c:{text:'This case shows where the resolution’s long-term criterion differs from many existing frameworks.',end:true}})]],
["Age as a criterion",["Using **age** directly is one of the most debated allocation questions. Supporters cite the **fair innings** idea and the fact that younger patients usually gain more life-years. Critics call age-based rules **ageist** and argue that chronological age is a poor proxy for individual prognosis.","Some frameworks allowed age only as a **tiebreaker** among patients with otherwise similar prognoses; many U.S. protocols were revised to remove categorical age cutoffs. The resolution does not mention age explicitly, but long-term survival correlates strongly with age, so expect this issue in nearly every round.","The affirmative should distance the resolution from age as such; the negative should show how age enters indirectly through life expectancy.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Age argument for which side?","buckets":["Affirmative","Negative"],"items":[["Younger patients usually gain more life-years",0],["Chronological age is a poor proxy for prognosis",1],["Fair innings: everyone deserves a full lifespan",0],["Age cutoffs were removed from many protocols",1],["Age used only as a tiebreaker",0],["Age-based rules reflect ageism",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Allocation principles fall into four families: treat equally (lottery, first come), favor the worst off (sickest, youngest), maximize benefits (most lives, life-years, prognosis) and social usefulness (instrumental value, reciprocity); no single principle suffices.','Emanuel’s COVID-19 framework: maximize benefits, treat people equally, promote instrumental value, prioritize the worst off; favor lottery over first come among similar patients; prioritize health care workers.','Prognosis tools (SOFA, MELD, EPTS/KDPI) are imperfect and can embed bias; long-term predictions are less accurate and more likely to disadvantage older and disabled people; AMA accepts likelihood of benefit, and civil rights guidance warns against age and disability discrimination.'],terms:[['Prognosis','A prediction of the likely course and outcome of a disease.'],['Instrumental value','Priority given to people whose work helps others, such as health workers.'],['Longevity matching','Matching donor organs expected to last longest with recipients expected to live longest.']],mcqs:[q('Which criterion belongs to the “maximize benefits” family?','Save the most life-years','Lottery','First come, first served','Sickest first','Utilitarian family.'),q('Which criterion belongs to “favoring the worst off”?','Sickest first','Save the most lives','Prognosis','Reciprocity','Worst off.'),q('Emanuel et al. recommended against which method?','First come, first served','Maximizing benefits','Prioritizing health workers','Random allocation among similar patients','It favors those with access.'),q('Among patients with similar prognoses, Emanuel et al. suggested:','Random allocation (lottery)','First come, first served','Choosing the wealthiest','Choosing by social status','Equality via lottery.'),q('SOFA scores measure:','Organ failure severity in ICU patients','Liver transplant urgency only','Kidney donor quality','Quality of life','Sequential Organ Failure Assessment.'),q('KDPI is used to describe:','Donor kidney quality and expected longevity','Heart rhythm','ICU beds','Vaccine supply','Longevity matching.'),q('Which concern does civil rights guidance raise about crisis standards?','Discrimination based on disability or age','Too many lotteries','Overuse of short-term criteria','Excessive transparency','Individualized assessment.'),q('Persad, Wertheimer and Emanuel concluded that:','No single principle is sufficient','Only lotteries are fair','Only prognosis matters','Rationing is unnecessary','Combine principles.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the main families of allocation criteria and explain where the resolution’s “greatest likelihood of long-term survival” criterion fits, including its strengths and limitations.',rubric:[['Families',['treat equally','lottery','worst off','sickest','maximize','life-years','instrumental','reciprocity']],['Fit',['prognosis','maximize benefits','long-term','life-years']],['Strengths',['benefit','stewardship','transparent','most lives','evidence']],['Limitations',['prediction','uncertain','sofa','bias','disability','age','civil rights','short-term']]],model:'Ethicists group allocation criteria into four families. Treating people equally includes lotteries and first come, first served; favoring the worst off includes giving priority to the sickest or youngest; maximizing total benefits includes saving the most lives, saving the most life-years and using prognosis; and promoting social usefulness includes instrumental value, such as prioritizing health care workers, and reciprocity, such as rewarding organ donors. The resolution’s criterion—greatest likelihood of long-term survival—belongs to the maximizing-benefits family because it combines prognosis with life-years: it gives scarce resources to those expected to gain the most years of life. Its strengths are that it uses each scarce ventilator, organ or drug where it is most likely to produce lasting benefit, it reflects the accepted professional criterion of likelihood of benefit and, if published in advance and applied by triage committees, it can be more transparent than hidden rationing through waiting lists. Its limitations are significant. Long-term predictions are less accurate than short-term ones, and tools like SOFA were not designed to forecast long-term survival. Prognostic tools can embed bias, and long-term criteria tend to disadvantage older adults and people with disabilities or chronic illness, which civil rights guidance during COVID-19 warned against. Leading frameworks such as Emanuel’s blend prognosis with other values, using lotteries among patients with similar outcomes rather than relying on one criterion.'},sources:[RULES,PERSAD,EMANUEL,AMA]};

export const BD5={title:'Real-world allocation cases',hook:'Allocation is not just a classroom puzzle. Transplant systems match organs every day, hospitals wrote ventilator triage plans during COVID-19, countries rationed HIV treatment and cancer drugs ran short in 2023. Real cases give your arguments concrete evidence—and they reveal both the promise and the pitfalls of survival-based rules.',objectives:['Explain how organ allocation balances urgency, benefit and fairness.','Describe crisis standards of care and COVID-19 triage controversies.','Analyze global examples such as HIV treatment scale-up.','Use real cases as evidence for either side.'],sections:[
['Organ transplantation',[
'In the United States, the **Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network (OPTN)** sets policies for allocating deceased-donor organs. Allocation balances **medical urgency**, **expected benefit**, **waiting time**, **blood and tissue compatibility**, **geography** and special priority for children. Liver allocation emphasizes urgency through the **MELD** score; kidney allocation since 2014 uses **longevity matching**, directing the kidneys expected to last longest to the candidates expected to live longest.',
'Transplantation shows **survival-based criteria already in use**—a strong affirmative example. But it also shows the **tension**: longevity matching was designed to apply to only a portion of kidneys, while urgency, waiting time and fairness still matter. Critics have raised concerns about disparities in referral and listing for patients of color and older adults.',
'Debaters can use transplantation to show that survival considerations can be ethical **when combined** with other principles and transparent rules.',
match('Organ allocation element and purpose',[['MELD score','Prioritizes urgency in liver allocation'],['Longevity matching','Pairs long-lasting kidneys with long-living recipients'],['Waiting time','Rewards time on the list'],['Pediatric priority','Gives children special consideration'],['Compatibility','Blood and tissue match'],['OPTN','Sets national allocation policy']])]],
['Crisis standards of care and COVID-19',[
'**Crisis standards of care** are plans for providing the best possible care when resources are overwhelmed. The National Academies’ 2009 guidance called for fairness, a duty to care, stewardship of resources, transparency and consistency. During COVID-19, many states and hospitals published **ventilator and ICU triage protocols**, often using SOFA scores and sometimes **comorbidities** or **age** as tiebreakers.',
'These protocols sparked controversy. Disability advocates filed complaints arguing that some excluded or deprioritized people with disabilities, and the HHS Office for Civil Rights worked with several states to revise plans to remove categorical exclusions and long-term survival judgments. Studies also raised concerns that SOFA scores could disadvantage Black patients because of underlying differences in kidney function measures linked to structural inequities.',
'This history is powerful for the **negative**: real attempts to use survival predictions led to discrimination concerns. The affirmative can respond that revised protocols still used **prognosis** and that the lesson is to implement criteria carefully, not to abandon benefit-based allocation.',
sort('Supports affirmative or negative?',['Affirmative','Negative'],[['Triage plans prioritized patients most likely to survive',0],['Civil rights complaints about disability exclusions',1],['Stewardship: use ventilators where they help most',0],['SOFA concerns for Black patients',1],['Transparent published protocols',0],['Revisions removed long-term survival judgments',1]])]],
['Global examples: HIV treatment and vaccines',[
'In 2003, the World Health Organization launched the **“3 by 5” initiative** to provide antiretroviral therapy to three million people with HIV in low- and middle-income countries by 2005. Because treatment was scarce, programs faced hard choices about **who** would receive it first—debates explored in **The Ethics of Health Care Rationing**, including concerns about fairness between rural and urban patients and about prioritizing those most likely to benefit.',
'During COVID-19 vaccine rollout, most countries prioritized **health care workers**, **older adults** and people with **high-risk conditions**—the groups at highest risk of severe illness and death. This shows that some resources are allocated to those **most at risk**, not those most likely to survive, because the goal was preventing deaths.',
'Global cases remind debaters that allocation goals differ: treatment, prevention and research may call for different criteria.',
chk('Why did many COVID-19 vaccine programs prioritize older adults?',['They had the highest risk of severe illness and death, so vaccinating them prevented the most deaths','They were most likely to survive without help','Younger people refused vaccines','Older adults paid more'],'Prevention targets those at highest risk.')]],
['Drug shortages and everyday rationing',[
'In **2023**, shortages of chemotherapy drugs such as **cisplatin** and **carboplatin** forced U.S. oncologists to decide which patients would receive full doses. Professional societies issued guidance prioritizing patients for whom the drugs were **curative** over those receiving them for palliation, and favoring regimens with fewer alternatives. This resembles the resolution: prioritizing patients for whom treatment offers the greatest chance of long-term survival.',
'Everyday rationing also includes **insurance formularies**, **prior authorization**, **specialist waiting lists** and **rural hospital closures**. These hidden forms of allocation often affect low-income and rural patients most.',
'The affirmative can cite curative-intent prioritization during drug shortages as a reasonable, real-world application. The negative can point out that palliative patients also deserve care and that everyday rationing already disadvantages vulnerable groups.',
match('Example and allocation lesson',[['Cisplatin shortage 2023','Prioritize curative intent'],['Insurance formularies','Hidden budget-based rationing'],['Rural hospital closures','Geographic inequity'],['Vaccine rollout','Priority to highest-risk groups'],['HIV 3 by 5','Fairness in global treatment scale-up'],['Ventilator triage','Prognosis-based crisis standards']])]],
['Using cases as evidence',[
'Cases make arguments concrete and memorable. For each case, prepare a **one-sentence summary**, the **key fact or statistic**, the **source and date** and a note on **which side it helps** and how the other side might respond. Store these on organized evidence cards in your printed folder.',
'Avoid overstating cases. If a protocol was revised, say so. If evidence is contested, acknowledge it. Judges reward **accurate, relevant** evidence and penalize claims that sound exaggerated or invented.',
'Choose a few strong cases rather than many weak ones, and explain **why** each case matters to the resolution.',
order('Evidence card for a case',['Case name and date','One-sentence summary','Key fact or statistic','Source','Which side it supports and why','Likely counterargument and response'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Using a case',{a:{text:'You are affirmative. The negative cites civil rights complaints about COVID-19 triage. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the strongest response?',choices:[['Acknowledge the concerns and argue the resolution can be implemented with individualized, unbiased assessments—as revised protocols still used prognosis','b','Correct.',true],['Claim the complaints never happened','b','Never deny accurate evidence.',false]]},b:{text:'Which affirmative case example supports survival-based rules?',choices:[['Curative-intent prioritization during the 2023 chemotherapy shortage','c','Correct.',true],['Vaccine priority for the highest-risk groups','c','That is a negative example.',false]]},c:{text:'Your rebuttal acknowledges the evidence while defending your position.',end:true}})]],
["Lessons learned from COVID-19",["The Lancet 2023 article on a **shared ethical framework** reflects on lessons from the pandemic. Common themes across the literature include the need to plan **before** crises, involve the **public** and affected communities in creating rules, use **transparent** and **consistent** criteria, reassess patients regularly, protect against **discrimination** and support clinicians facing moral distress.","Many hospitals never had to activate formal crisis triage because they expanded capacity, transferred patients or delayed elective care. This suggests that **preventing scarcity**—through stockpiles, staffing and surge planning—can reduce the need for hard choices.","Both teams can use these lessons: the affirmative to argue that clear, pre-planned criteria are necessary, and the negative to argue that criteria must avoid long-term judgments and that preventing scarcity matters more.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Lessons for allocation planning","steps":["Plan criteria before a crisis","Engage the public and affected communities","Publish transparent, consistent rules","Assess and reassess patients individually","Protect against discrimination","Support clinicians and expand capacity"]},"Cite the official Lancet reference when discussing these lessons; test questions may draw on it."]]
],takeaways:['Organ allocation (OPTN) balances urgency (MELD), benefit (kidney longevity matching), waiting time, compatibility and pediatric priority—survival is used but combined with other principles.','COVID-19 crisis standards used SOFA scores and sometimes age or comorbidities; civil rights complaints led to revisions removing categorical exclusions and long-term survival judgments—strong negative evidence.','WHO’s 3 by 5 HIV initiative and vaccine rollouts (priority to highest-risk groups) show different goals; the 2023 chemotherapy shortage prioritized curative intent; build accurate evidence cards for each case.'],terms:[['Crisis standards of care','Plans for providing the best care possible when resources are overwhelmed.'],['MELD score','A score that prioritizes liver transplant candidates by urgency.'],['3 by 5 initiative','WHO’s goal of treating three million people with HIV by 2005.']],mcqs:[q('Which organization sets U.S. deceased-donor organ allocation policy?','OPTN','FDA','CDC','WHO','Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network.'),q('Kidney longevity matching pairs:','Long-lasting kidneys with recipients expected to live longest','Any kidney with the first patient','Kidneys with the sickest patients only','Kidneys with the wealthiest patients','Survival-based.'),q('What did WHO’s 3 by 5 initiative aim to do?','Provide antiretroviral therapy to three million people by 2005','Vaccinate five million children','Build three hospitals','Eradicate polio by 2005','HIV treatment.'),q('During the 2023 chemotherapy shortage, guidance prioritized:','Patients receiving curative-intent treatment','Patients in alphabetical order','Only older patients','No one','Greatest benefit.'),q('Most COVID-19 vaccine programs first prioritized:','Health care workers and high-risk groups','The youngest healthy adults','Random lottery for all','Only athletes','Highest risk.'),q('Crisis standards of care are:','Plans for care when resources are overwhelmed','Daily staffing schedules','Insurance contracts','Vaccine labels','Emergency planning.'),q('Which concern led some states to revise COVID-19 triage plans?','Disability discrimination complaints','Too few rules','Excess ventilators','Lack of SOFA scores','Civil rights.'),q('Which is everyday implicit rationing?','Insurance prior authorization','A published organ policy','A triage committee protocol','A lottery','Hidden allocation.')],frq:{prompt:'Choose two real-world allocation cases and explain how each could be used as evidence by the affirmative or the negative on this year’s resolution.',rubric:[['Case one',['transplant','optn','longevity matching','meld','covid','sofa','shortage']],['Case two',['vaccine','3 by 5','hiv','cisplatin','civil rights','crisis standards']],['Side and reasoning',['affirmative','negative','supports','because','survival','benefit','discrimination']],['Accuracy and response',['acknowledge','counterargument','source','revised','combined']]],model:'My first case is kidney transplant allocation. Since 2014, the OPTN kidney policy has used longevity matching, directing the kidneys expected to last longest to candidates with the longest expected post-transplant survival, while still considering waiting time, compatibility and pediatric priority. The affirmative can use this as evidence that survival-based allocation is already accepted in a carefully regulated system that aims to make the most of scarce organs. The negative can respond that longevity matching applies only to a portion of kidneys and is combined with other fairness principles, so it does not show that long-term survival should be the main criterion for all scarce resources. My second case is COVID-19 ventilator triage. Many states published crisis standards of care using SOFA scores and sometimes comorbidities or age to predict survival. The negative can use this history to show that attempts to rank patients by predicted survival led to complaints that protocols discriminated against people with disabilities, and that the HHS Office for Civil Rights worked with states to remove categorical exclusions and long-term life expectancy judgments; studies also raised concerns that SOFA could disadvantage Black patients. The affirmative can acknowledge these concerns but argue that revised protocols still relied on individualized prognosis, so the lesson is to implement survival criteria carefully rather than abandon them. Using both cases accurately shows judges command of the evidence on each side.'},sources:[RULES,OPTN,NASEM_CSC,EMANUEL]};
